-- title:
--   Bounded rigidification degree from bounded transport exponent
-- statement:
--   The statement is made inside a very large frame of fixed data, which is described first; the hypotheses are grouped and the groups are named, and the conclusion is then stated in full.
--
--   Arithmetic data. Primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$, and a nonzero natural number $N$ with $N$ squarefree, $r \nmid N$ and $\bar r \nmid N$.
--
--   Coefficient data. A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring, an irreducible element $\pi \in \mathcal O$, completeness of $\mathcal O$ for the $\pi$-adic topology, the requirement that $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ have exactly $r$ elements and that $(r) = (\pi)$ in $\mathcal O$, and a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ realised as the fraction field of $\mathcal O$. Further, a characteristic-zero domain $Onr$ over $\mathcal O$ with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $Fr$, such that $Onr$ is $\pi$-adically complete, $(\pi)Onr$ is maximal, every element of $Onr$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $(\pi)$, every monic polynomial over $Onr$ of positive degree has a root modulo $(\pi)$, and $Fr(x) \equiv x^{r} \pmod{(\pi)}$ for all $x$; thus $Onr$ plays the role of the completion of a maximal unramified extension with its Frobenius. Finally a homomorphism $vdet$ from $\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ to $\mathbb Z$ written multiplicatively, characterised by $vdet(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g$ is a unit of $\mathcal O$ times $\pi^{n}$.
--
--   Quaternionic data and moduli. Rationals $a,b$ with `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b r rbar`, i.e. $a > 0$ or $b > 0$ and the finite places at which $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ is a division algebra are exactly those above $r$ and $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda$ in this algebra. A scheme $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec} \mathcal O$ with a point-assignment `pt` on fake elliptic curves with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ structure, and the hypothesis `h𝒳` that $(\mathcal X, f, \mathrm{pt})$ is a coarse moduli space for such curves (invariance under isomorphism, compatibility with base change, bijectivity on geometric points, and the universal property). A level $n \geq 3$ prime to $r$, to $\bar r$ and to $N$, a scheme $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with `ptF` and the hypothesis `hM` that it is a fine moduli space for curves with full level-$n$ structure, a group $G$ acting on $M$ by automorphisms via $\rho$ with labels $\chi$ satisfying `IsLevelTwistAction` (the action is over the base, realises twisting of the level structure by $\chi$, and $\chi$ is multiplicative and bijective modulo $n$). A morphism $p : M \to \mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$ which is $G$-invariant and compatible with the two point-assignments. For every prime $\ell$ distinct from $r$ and $\bar r$, a scheme $\mathcal Y_\ell$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with `ptT` which is a coarse moduli space for curves with extra level-$\ell$ structure, together with two degeneracy maps $d_0, d_1 : \mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathcal X$ over the base, $d_0$ forgetting the extra structure and $d_1$ sending a point to the target of the corresponding $\ell$-isogeny. Endomorphisms $ar, arbar$ of $\mathcal X$ over the base inducing the Atkin–Lehner involutions at $r$ and at $\bar r$ on points.
--
--   Definite companion data. Rationals $a_1,b_1$ with `IsDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt rbar`, i.e. $a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$ and the finite ramified places are exactly those above $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda_1$, an Eichler order $R_1$ of level $N$ contained in $\Lambda_1$; an adelic unit $n_1$ in the prime Hecke set of $R_1$ at $r$ such that $R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ is an Eichler order of level $Nr$; an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map $\iota_0$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ into $M_2(K_0)$; a finite place $v$ above $r$. A subgroup $\Gamma_t$ of units characterised as the subgroup of units integral away from $v$ with respect to $R_1$. For each prime $\ell \neq r,\bar r$, elements $s_\ell$ (global) and $sf_\ell$ (adelic) such that $sf_\ell$ agrees with $s_\ell$ away from $r$, is trivial at $r$, differs from the diagonal image of $\ell$ by an element of the level Hecke set at $\ell$ (when $\ell \mid N$) or of the prime Hecke set at $\ell$ (otherwise) for the order $R_1 \cap n_1R_1n_1^{-1}$, and such that $\mathrm{nrd}(s_\ell) = \ell$; and the congruence subgroups $\Gamma_{t,\ell} = \Gamma_t \cap s_\ell \Gamma_t s_\ell^{-1}$. An element $\bar w$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\Gamma_t$.
--
--   Formal data at $r$. The hypothesis $h\Lambda_{\mathbb Z}$ that all rational integers lie in $\Lambda$, and a coordinate map $coord$ on $\Lambda$ with values in $\mathbb Z_{r^2}^{\,2}$ satisfying `IsOrderCoord` (additive, unital, multiplicative for the twisted product involving Frobenius, injective, with dense image and the trace condition). A fake elliptic curve $A_0$ over $Onr/(\pi)$, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$ over $Onr/(\pi)$ of height $4$, formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of dimension $2$ along the unit section of $A_0$, and the hypothesis that $A_0$ is a formal module via $coord$, $X_0$ and $\theta_0$. Families $e, e'$ of endomorphisms of $A_0$ indexed by $\Gamma_t$, with $e\gamma$ over the base and with degree exponents $deg$, subject to the groups of hypotheses `hE1` ($(e\gamma, e'\gamma)$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{deg\gamma}$ preserving the level structure), `hE1mul` (multiplicativity of $\gamma \mapsto e\gamma$ up to multiplication by powers of $r$), `hE1sc` (scalars $\gamma = c$ give $e\gamma$ equal to multiplication by $c$ up to powers of $r$), `hE1'` (conversely, if $e\gamma$ is a multiplication up to a power of $r$ then $\gamma$ is a scalar), `hE2` (for every injective ring map $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $\mathcal O_D$-action on $X_0$ into $M_2(K_0)$ there is $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ such that each $e\gamma$ is induced on formal coordinates by an element $\varepsilon$ of that centraliser with $E_0\varepsilon = r^{k_\gamma}\, g\, \iota_0(\gamma)\, g^{-1}$), `hE3` (each full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$ is moved by $e\gamma$ by a label $lab(\gamma) \in \Lambda$, multiplicative and compatible with scalars modulo $n$) and `hE4` (over an algebraically closed field, every $r$-power-degree isogeny pair of a fake elliptic curve pulled back from $A_0$ which preserves the level comes from some $e\gamma$ up to powers of $r$).
--
--   Optimal embedding data. A submodule $R_2 \leq R_1$ which is an order, such that every element of $R_1$ becomes an element of $R_2$ after multiplication by a power of $r$, together with endomorphisms $\hat e$ of $A_0$ indexed by $R_2$ and over the base, subject to the groups `hE5a` (each $\hat e x$ is a homomorphism for the group law, commutes with the $\Lambda$-action and preserves the level), `hE5b1`–`hE5b3` (unit, multiplicativity, and agreement with multiplication by integers), `hE5c` ($\hat e(\bar x) \circ \hat e(x)$ is multiplication by $\mathrm{nrd}(x)$), `hE5d` (compatibility of $\hat e$ with $e$ on $r$-power multiples of elements of $\Gamma_t$ up to powers of $r$) and `hE5e` (a simultaneous version of `hE2` for both $\Gamma_t$ and $R_2$, with one common conjugating matrix $g$).
--
--   Drinfeld-side data. A ring map $\iota$ from $\mathbb Z_{r^2}$ to $Onr$; a special formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $\Phi$ over $Onr/(r)$ of height $4$ with respect to $\iota$; a moduli package $MD$ over $Onr$ which is a Zariski sheaf; a map $\eta$ sending rigidified objects to points of $MD$, subject to the three clauses of `hη`: over Noetherian bases $\eta$ separates admissible rigidified objects exactly up to `Rigidified.IsIsomorphic`, $\eta$ commutes with base change along ring maps, and every point of $MD$ is, locally on a distinguished open cover, in the image of $\eta$ on admissible objects. An injective map $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $\mathcal O_D$-action on $\Phi$ into $M_2(K_0)$ whose image is, up to a fixed power $r^{m}$, the set of matrices over $\mathcal O$ (`hE₀`). A natural bijection $eD$ between the functor of $MD$-points with Frobenius label and the product of the functor corepresented by $Onr$ with Deligne's functor $\Omega$ of lines in lattice base changes, together with the clauses `hnatD` (naturality), `hbijD` (bijectivity), `hfstD` (the first component is the structure map), `hGLD` ($\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$-equivariance, matching the action of $g$ on $MD$-points with the twisted action on $\Omega$-points, where the Frobenius twist is by $-vdet(g)$ and the datum is pulled back along $g^{-1}$), `hPiD` and `hPiexD` (the $\pi$-translate operation corresponds to a single Frobenius twist of the first component, and always exists).
--
--   Comparison data. A ring map $\kappa$ from $Onr/(\pi)$ to $Onr/(r)$ compatible with the quotient maps, a natural number $n_0$ and a series $\beta_0$ exhibiting $\beta_0$ as an isogeny of height $4n_0$ from $\Phi$ to the base change of $X_0$ along $\kappa$. A full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$. Atkin–Lehner data at $\bar r$ in the fibre: a curve $A_{0w}$, maps $aw, aw'$ exhibiting $A_{0w}$ as the Atkin–Lehner quotient of $A_0$ at $\bar r$, an $r^{k_w}$-isogeny pair $bw, bw'$ between $A_{0w}$ and $A_0$ preserving the level, and `hJOINTw`, which asserts that the matrix $g$ of `hE2` may be chosen so as to realise simultaneously all $e\gamma$ and the composite $aw \circ bw$ (in diagrammatic order $aw$ followed by $bw$), the latter corresponding to $\iota_0(\bar w)$ up to a power of $r$.
--
--   The transport functor $\Xi$. A map $\Xi$ assigning to every Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent, every $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi : Onr \to B$, and every rigidified curve $x = (E,\rho)$ over $B$ — that is, a fake elliptic curve $E$ over $B$ together with a `Rigidification`, consisting of the reduction $E_b$ of $E$ modulo $\pi$, a fake elliptic curve $A_b$ over $B/(\pi)$ pulled back from $A_0$ along $\psi$, an exponent $d$ and an isogeny pair $\varphi, \varphi'$ of degree $r^{d}$ between $E_b$ and $A_b$ preserving the level — a point of the $MD$-point functor over $B$. It is subject to `hΞleg` (over a connected base the Frobenius label of $\Xi$ is a twist of $\psi$), `hΞnat` (naturality in $B$ along pullbacks of rigidifications), `hΞiso` (invariance under isomorphisms of rigidified curves compatible with the two legs up to powers of $r$) and `hΞdef`, the defining clause: over a connected Noetherian base with $r$ nilpotent, for any formal coordinates $(X,\theta)$ making $E$ a formal module via $coord$, there exist $j \leq 1$ and an admissible rigidified object $t$ with $t.X = X$, related to $\rho$ by `IsRigTransport` with respect to $\theta_0, \kappa, \beta_0$ and the twist exponent $j$, such that $\Xi(x)$ is the point with label $Fr^{-j}\psi$ and underlying $MD$-point $\eta(t)$. Finally a matrix $g_0 \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ and two equivariance clauses: `heqΓ`, which says that if two rigidifications $\rho, \rho'$ of the same curve are related by translation by $e\gamma$ then $\Xi$ of the two rigidified curves are related by the action of $r^{c} \cdot g_0 \iota_0(\gamma) g_0^{-1}$ for some $c \in \mathbb Z$; and `heqW`, the analogous clause for the Atkin–Lehner map at $\bar r$ (with $\bar r$ invertible on the base), the acting matrix being $r^{c} \cdot g_0 \iota_0(\bar w) g_0^{-1}$.
--
--   Conclusion. For every natural number $N_b$ there exists a natural number $d_N$ with the following property. Let $S$ be a Noetherian local Artinian ring with algebraically closed residue field, equipped with an $\mathcal O$-algebra structure for which $\pi$ is nilpotent, let $\psi_S : Onr \to S$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra map, let $x$ be a rigidified curve over $S$ with respect to $A_0$ and $\psi_S$, and let $t$ be a rigidified object for $\Phi$ over $S$. Assume that $t$ is admissible with respect to $\iota$ and the Frobenius label of $\Xi(x)$, that $\eta$ applied to $t$ (with that label and the accompanying nilpotence witness) equals the $MD$-point component of $\Xi(x)$, and that the exponent $t.n$ is at most $N_b$. Then there exists a rigidification $\rho'$ of the same curve $x.1$ over $S$ such that both of the following hold: the degree exponent $\rho'.d$ is at most $d_N$, and $\rho'$ is equivalent to the given rigidification $x.2$ in the sense of `Rigidification.Equiv` relative to $h\Lambda_{\mathbb Z}$, that is, there are comparison morphisms between the reductions and between the auxiliary curves, each a pullback along the identity and compatible with the two legs, and natural numbers $i, j$ such that the two isogenies to $A_b$ agree after composing with multiplication by $r^{i}$ and $r^{j}$ respectively.
--
--   This is the quantitative step in the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison which converts a bound on the transport exponent of a rigidified object on the Drinfeld side into a bound on the degree exponent of a rigidification of the corresponding fake elliptic curve, up to equivalence by $r$-power scalars, over an Artinian local base with algebraically closed residue field. It feeds the version of the same bound formulated for points lying in an edge chart of the Drinfeld upper half plane.
